      </w:r>
      <w:r>
        <w:t>For decades the city knew about such waves only after the fact. The loop detectors under the asphalt, one every half kilometre, count vehicles and measure their speed, but between two detectors the road is dark. A wave that forms between them is invisible until it reaches the next loop.</w:t>
      </w:r>
    </w:p>
    <w:p>
      <w:pPr>
        <w:pStyle w:val="Heading2"/>
      </w:pPr>
      <w:r>
        <w:t>Filling the gaps</w:t>
      </w:r>
    </w:p>
    <w:p>
      <w:r>
        <w:t>The new system adds a second source: anonymous speed reports from navigation apps and delivery fleets, the so-called floating cars. They are few, a few percent of all vehicles, but they cover every metre of road. A mathematical model of traffic flow, the same one engineers have used since the 1950s, ties the two sources together.</w:t>
      </w:r>
    </w:p>
    <w:p>
      <w:r>
        <w:drawing>
          <wp:anchor xmlns:wp="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/drawingml/2006/wordprocessingDrawing" allowOverlap="1" behindDoc="0" distB="0" distL="108000" distR="114300" distT="0" layoutInCell="1" locked="0" relativeHeight="251662336" simplePos="0">
