                  <pic:nvPicPr>
                    <pic:cNvPr descr="A ring of twelve sensors" id="0" name="sensors.png"/>
                    <pic:cNvPicPr/>
                  </pic:nvPicPr>
                  <pic:blipFill>
                    <a:blip xmlns:r="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships" r:embed="rId7"/>
                    <a:stretch>
                      <a:fillRect/>
                    </a:stretch>
                  </pic:blipFill>
                  <pic:spPr>
                    <a:xfrm>
                      <a:off x="0" y="0"/>
                      <a:ext cx="1512000" cy="1512000"/>
                    </a:xfrm>
                    <a:prstGeom prst="rect">
                      <a:avLst/>
                    </a:prstGeom>
                  </pic:spPr>
                </pic:pic>
              </a:graphicData>
            </a:graphic>
          </wp:anchor>
        </w:drawing>
      </w:r>
      <w:r>
        <w:t>The model divides the road into cells of five hundred metres and predicts, every ten seconds, how many vehicles each cell holds. Where a detector or a floating car reports, the prediction is corrected. Where nothing reports, the model carries the picture forward. The result is a live map of density along the whole ring, updated faster than the traffic changes.</w:t>
      </w:r>
    </w:p>
    <w:p>
      <w:pPr>
        <w:pStyle w:val="Heading2"/>
      </w:pPr>
      <w:r>
        <w:t>What the numbers say</w:t>
      </w:r>
    </w:p>
    <w:p>
      <w:r>
        <w:t>Over four test weeks the combined estimate was a third more accurate than the detectors alone, and almost twice as accurate in congestion, exactly where it matters most.</w:t>
      </w:r>
    </w:p>
